SIGNIFICANCE Protein aggregation is a physicochemical process that underlies many diseases and disorders, such as Alzheimer’s or Huntington’s disease. Here, we study experimentally and theoretically the effect of a sharp decrease of diffusivity in the aggregation dynamics, such as the one that could happen in the cell because of the presence of obstacles. We find that two different timescales are important in setting the size of large aggregates, and we give an estimate of the size of the aggregate at which this dramatic change in behavior occurs, which could be not exclusive to protein aggregation but also affect many other intracellular processes.
